'''2.''' The oil or spirit of turpentine; turps: an ordinary but less precise use.—'''Aleppo turpentine''', {{smaller|an article resembling, but not equal to, the Bordeaux turpentine, obtained in Provence from ''Pinus Halepensis.''}}—'''Canada turpentine''', {{smaller|Canada balsam. (See ''balsam.'') During the American civil war, turpentine of the common sort was obtained from the Canadian red pine, ''Pinus resinosa.''}}—'''Carpathian turpentine''', {{smaller|usually called ''Carpathian balsam'', a turpentine from the Swiss stone-pine, ''Pinus Cembra.''}}—'''Chian turpentine''', {{smaller|the product of the turpentine-tree (which see), obtained by incision. It is of a feebly aromatic and terebinthinous flavor, not bitter or acrid, and of a characteristic pleasantly aromatic and terebinthinous scent. It was formerly of medicinal repute, then fell nearly into disuse, but latterly has been used with some success for cancer. Also ''Cyprian'' or ''Scio turpentine.''}}—'''Hungarian turpentine''', {{smaller|the product of the dwarf pine, ''Pinus Pumilio'', usually called ''Hungarian balsam'', an article scarcely met with in commerce. Its essential oil is used as an inhalant in throat-diseases.}}—'''Larch turpentine.''' {{smaller|Same as ''Venetian turpentine.''}}—'''Mineral turpentine''', {{smaller|a deodorized benzoin used in painting as a substi-}}

